  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/hypoptopoma-gulare-2/",
      "html": "<p>The genus Hypoptopoma comprises small to medium sized loricariids. The largest species known becomes about 10 cm long. At first glance they look much like an Otocinclus, but larger. Typical for members of Hypoptopoma is the position of the eyes. They are placed at the side of the head in such a way that the fish can look over almost 360° when it sits on a plant. Among the loricariids this feature is found only in Hypoptopoma and Oxyropsis. The latter, however, has a flattened caudal peduncle, while in Hypoptopoma the caudal peduncle is oval in cross section.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/hypoptopoma-gulare-2/",
      "html": "<p>Determining the species of Hypoptopoma is tricky, although a thorough taxonomic revision of the genus was published in 2010. A species from Peru is obtained quite often and is thought to be Hypoptopoma gulare.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/hypoptopoma-gulare-2/",
      "html": "<p>Peru. The species was scientifically described from the surroundings of Loreto, where collectors of ornamental fish often work.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/hypoptopoma-gulare-2/",
      "html": "<p>Maintaining Hypoptopoma is comparable to that of the much better known Otocinclus, but the larger Hypoptopoma of course need more food.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/hypoptopoma-gulare-2/",
      "html": "<p>If not enough algae grow in the tank, feed the fish with food tablets or slices of vegetables to make sure they find enough to eat.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/hypoptopoma-gulare-2/",
      "html": "<p>Hypoptopoma are completely peaceful towards conspecifics and other fish.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/hypoptopoma-gulare-2/",
      "html": "<p>It is not possible to distinguish the sexes externally.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/hypoptopoma-gulare-2/",
      "html": "<p>They are open spawners; the male guards the eggs until they hatch.</p>"
    }
  ],
